00:36:15.129 --> 00:36:16.670
BRYAN MCKAY: There
really is one more

00:36:16.670 --> 00:36:19.130
that I want to add because,
Jayanth, as you know,

00:36:19.130 --> 00:36:22.430
after the application was built,
tested we sat down with some

00:36:22.430 --> 00:36:26.120
of the engineers and we had some
really interesting discussions.

00:36:26.120 --> 00:36:27.970
So I would say, first
of all, everything

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:31.070
you heard about the
core capabilities is how

00:36:31.070 --> 00:36:32.312
extremely important.

00:36:32.312 --> 00:36:33.770
I mean, it's what
allowed us to get

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:35.754
the projects to where it is.

00:36:35.754 --> 00:36:37.670
But then, when we sat
down with the engineers,

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:40.317
we had an opportunity to
sit side by side with them.

00:36:40.317 --> 00:36:41.900
And I would recommend
this is the best

00:36:41.900 --> 00:36:45.690
practice for all of you who
take on a project like this.

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:48.860
Sit with your users, have
them do search tasks,

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:51.110
because you will learn some
really interesting things.

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.000
But don't tell
them how to do it.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:55.830
Ask them just to sit
down and find something.

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:58.160
And what we learned
in the process was--

00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:01.200
and, actually, when the
GIF plays in a few minutes,

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:02.690
you'll see this.

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:04.956
And so we would
have a user sit down

00:37:04.956 --> 00:37:06.830
they say, I need to find
a lab report, right?

00:37:06.830 --> 00:37:09.140
So the first thing
that they do is,

00:37:09.140 --> 00:37:13.370
they start thinking about it
as though they are searching

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:14.540
in their legacy system.

00:37:14.540 --> 00:37:16.550
They think about it
as a database query.

00:37:16.550 --> 00:37:18.710
And so they start to
structure their query,

00:37:18.710 --> 00:37:23.310
lab report, and then blender
noise test, for example,

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:25.880
a model shop request,
blender noise,

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:27.860
whatever that search was.

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:29.890
And what we discovered
in that was,

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:31.924
they're framing a
question, they don't really

00:37:31.924 --> 00:37:33.590
know how to use the
search bar, and then

00:37:33.590 --> 00:37:35.750
how to use the search
bar, we all know Google.

00:37:35.750 --> 00:37:39.500
They don't really know in the
context of work and that task

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:41.160
how do you use Google.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:44.540
And so we said to them,
what if you could just

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.480
type in "Show me
all lab requests"

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:49.320
for blender noise test?

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.960
They're like, yes, that's it.

00:37:50.960 --> 00:37:52.740
That's exactly
what we want to do.

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:55.425
And so what we learned is, they
all want to ask a question,

00:37:55.425 --> 00:37:57.050
they don't want to
learn how to search,

00:37:57.050 --> 00:38:00.290
they don't want to learn another
structured UI advanced search,

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:02.510
they just want to
ask the question.

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:06.080
And so the key there is,
that's the next thing

00:38:06.080 --> 00:38:07.280
that we'll be working on.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:09.980
We are going to start
introducing that ability

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:12.440
so that those users
can just ask questions.

00:38:12.440 --> 00:38:14.150
But that leads to
another thing, and that

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:16.400
is you really have
to know your data,

00:38:16.400 --> 00:38:18.034
you really have to
know your users,

00:38:18.034 --> 00:38:20.700
you have to know something about
the questions they want to ask.

00:38:20.700 --> 00:38:23.120
And therefore, make sure
you have all the metadata

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:25.010
and the right bits and
pieces in the index,

00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:29.330
so they will get the
answers to their questions.

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:32.220
ANDREW LEWIS: I'd say to chime
in on that, one of the things--

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:34.470
one of the challenges that
we had with the old system,

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:37.710
not the GSA, but
the pre-GSA days,

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:40.860
is a lot of these databases
all grew up organically, right?

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:42.390
So you have a lab
reports database,

00:38:42.390 --> 00:38:44.670
and you have another
Oracle database here,

00:38:44.670 --> 00:38:46.080
and then you'd have Windchill.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.930
And all of those search
experiences for the end user

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.170
were all different.

00:38:50.170 --> 00:38:51.905
So you couldn't just
go in and search,

00:38:51.905 --> 00:38:53.280
you had to go,
oh, wait a minute,

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:57.480
this one uses keywords and
this one uses asterisks.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:59.280
And so folks
actually had to think

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:03.620
about how to find the data
and not just go find the data.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:05.370
We had a really good
story of somebody

00:39:05.370 --> 00:39:07.890
that started at
Whirlpool and her job

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:13.680
was to design lab reports or
lab tests, sorry, for laundry.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:17.190
And she estimated it took her
about a year to get good at it

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:18.930
just to find all
the information,

00:39:18.930 --> 00:39:22.020
find all of the systems,
understand what other folks had

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:23.050
done in this space.

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:26.280
And so from an employee
experience and the productivity

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:29.160
standpoint, just putting this
on top of all that information

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.584
is just insanely powerful.

00:39:31.584 --> 00:39:33.250
JAYANTH MYSORE: Thank
you so much, yeah.

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:35.000
And for some reason,
it took a few seconds

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.580
for the Whirlpool demo to
start up, now that's been done.

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:41.314
It's a big filing system.

00:39:41.314 --> 00:39:42.730
CHAD JOHNSON:
Jayanth, one thing I

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:45.310
wanted to add was, a lot of
people have come up to me

00:39:45.310 --> 00:39:47.680
and asked me, why
do I need this?

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:49.670
Why do I want this?

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:54.580
And I start asking them about
their own environment at work

00:39:54.580 --> 00:39:57.520
and how many different
places content lives.

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:00.890
They-- oh, yeah, I search
Gmail, it works great.

00:40:00.890 --> 00:40:03.700
Yeah, but maybe it was
in Drive or-- wait,

00:40:03.700 --> 00:40:05.290
was it in SharePoint?

00:40:05.290 --> 00:40:07.030
I can't remember.

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:10.750
And providing a single
search interface

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:13.990
for all of the
repositories, that

00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:19.690
has really been the mantra
of enterprise search.

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:22.510
But it fails if we
don't index everything.

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:26.830
It fails if we have a room with
the lights off with shadows,

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:29.770
where our search doesn't
see into that part

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:32.470
of the enterprise.

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:34.520
I run a search and I
don't get any results,

00:40:34.520 --> 00:40:37.720
I don't know if that's
because I ran the wrong search

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:39.940
or it was because
we aren't indexing

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:41.922
that part of the repository.

00:40:41.922 --> 00:40:43.630
So Cloud Search is
one of the first tools

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:48.730
I've seen that has the
scale and capacity to index

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:51.850
not just some of what an
enterprise has, but all of what

00:40:51.850 --> 00:40:52.647
an enterprise has.

00:40:52.647 --> 00:40:54.730
And that's how we'll be
successful with Enterprise

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:57.032
Search.

00:40:57.032 --> 00:40:58.490
JAYANTH MYSORE:
It's great to know.

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:00.073
One thing we have
done is to make sure

00:41:00.073 --> 00:41:03.050
that I like to use
terms like limitless

00:41:03.050 --> 00:41:05.620
and note be humble about it
because it's truly limitless.

00:41:05.620 --> 00:41:08.036
How much [INAUDIBLE] to put
any limits on how much you can

00:41:08.036 --> 00:41:10.370
index, how much you
index is up to you.

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:12.950
The whole model has been set
up such that one does not

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:14.971
have to think about
number of VMs to buy,

00:41:14.971 --> 00:41:16.970
number of nodes to spin
up and things like that.

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:18.620
It should simply be,
hey, I want to make

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:20.920
that new repository also
available, write the connector,

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:21.586
and you're done.

00:41:21.586 --> 00:41:22.850
It starts coming in.

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:24.800
And that's also why we
put in a lot of effort

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:27.140
into the complex accurate
reflection effort,

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:29.660
so that, again,
that's an area that

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:31.490
is so sophisticated
wherein we felt

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:34.050
if we put the owners on
anything but the technology,

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:35.550
nobody would actually do it.

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:37.830
So that's the-- those
are all [INAUDIBLE]

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:41.455
gone to enabling that sort of
an incentive for most customers.

00:41:41.455 --> 00:41:42.580
TOMMATHEW THOMAS: You know?

00:41:42.580 --> 00:41:46.190
I would echo Chad's comments
on just getting all the content

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:47.790
sources up.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:50.504
We're really excited
that Lumapps is taking up

00:41:50.504 --> 00:41:51.920
partnership with
you because we're

00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:54.120
making that journey with them.

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:56.789
But I think when we presented
this in front of our end users,

00:41:56.789 --> 00:41:58.580
one of the comments
that they made to Jason

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:00.470
and the team was that
they were impressed

00:42:00.470 --> 00:42:03.890
by the speed of the
search and how fast they

00:42:03.890 --> 00:42:05.330
could get to their information.

00:42:05.330 --> 00:42:07.610
So to me, I think
what's exciting is

00:42:07.610 --> 00:42:11.750
that we will provide a
front end to the users that

00:42:11.750 --> 00:42:15.680
has the performance that they're
looking for to find insights.

00:42:15.680 --> 00:42:17.450
I think that's
going to be the key.

00:42:17.450 --> 00:42:19.324
JAYANTH MYSORE: Thank
you so much all of you.

00:42:19.324 --> 00:42:22.060
